lesson: 174
title: Too many or too few: surplus and shortage
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: If each child gets 3 pencils, 5 are left over; if each gets 4, 2 are missing. The number of pencils is the same both times.
explain: Giving one more pencil to every child uses up the 5 extra pencils and the 2 that are missing: 5 + 2 = 7 more pencils.
explain: One more pencil for each child needs 7 more pencils in all, so there are 7 / 1 = 7 children.
explain: Children = (surplus + shortage) / (difference per child); the pencils are 7 × 3 + 5 = 26.
vars: a=name c=3..20 p=2..7 g=1..4 s=2..15
let: q = p + g
let: d = c * g - s
need: d >= 2
need: c * q <= 200
answer: c
exercise: A teacher gives pencils to the children. If each child gets {p} pencils, {s} pencils are left over. If each child gets {q} pencils, {d} pencils are missing. How many children are there?
exercise: {a} shares candies among friends. With {p} candies each, {s} candies are left. With {q} candies each, {a.he} would be {d} candies short. How many friends are there?
exercise: Apples are put into baskets. If each basket gets {p} apples, {s} apples are left over. If each basket gets {q} apples, {d} more apples are needed. How many baskets are there?
exercise: If every child takes {p} books, {s} books remain. If every child takes {q} books, there are not enough: {d} books are missing. How many children are there?
exercise: Some students hire boats. If every boat carries {p} students, {s} students have no place. If every boat carries {q} students, {d} places stay empty. How many boats are there?
exercise: A gardener plants trees in rows. With {p} trees in each row, {s} trees are left. With {q} trees in each row, {d} trees are lacking. How many rows are there?
test: {d} stickers short — that is what happens when every pupil should get {q} stickers. When each pupil gets {p}, there are {s} stickers to spare. How many pupils are in the group?
test: A class goes to a museum. If {q} pupils sit at each table in the café, {d} seats are free; if only {p} sit at each table, {s} pupils must stand. How many tables are in the café?
test: {a} packs {p} eggs in each box and has {s} eggs left. Had {a.he} packed {q} eggs in each box, {a.he} would have needed {d} more eggs. The number of boxes is the same. How many boxes are there?
test: The coach hands out balls. Giving {p} balls to each team leaves {s} balls in the bag; giving {q} to each team would need {d} balls more than the coach has. How many teams are there?
exercise: When every child gets {p} stickers, {s} stickers are left over; when every child gets {q}, the teacher is {d} stickers short. How many children are in the group?
exercise: A coach in Ohio hands out cones to the teams. {p} cones per team leaves {s} cones spare; {q} cones per team would need {d} more cones. How many teams are there?
exercise: {a} lives in Melbourne. {a.He} puts {p} oranges in every bag and has {s} oranges left. With {q} oranges in every bag, {a.he} would be {d} oranges short. How many bags are there?
exercise: Sharing with {p} apiece leaves {s} over; sharing with {q} apiece leaves {d} too few. How many people share?
exercise: Campers in Canada sleep in tents. If {p} campers go in each tent, {s} campers have nowhere to sleep. If {q} campers go in each tent, there are {d} empty places. How many tents are there?
exercise: A teacher in Dublin hands out {p} crayons apiece and has {s} crayons left. To hand out {q} crayons apiece, she would need {d} more crayons. How many pupils are there?
exercise: Cards are dealt to the players. Dealing {p} cards to every player leaves {s} cards; dealing {q} cards to every player, the dealer is short of {d} cards. How many players are there?
exercise: A farmer in Kent puts eggs into cartons. With {p} eggs per carton, {s} eggs are left over. With {q} eggs per carton, {d} more eggs would be needed. How many cartons are there?
test: How many tables are there at a wedding in Auckland? If the guests sit {p} to a table, {s} guests have no seat; if they sit {q} to a table, {d} seats are empty.
test: {a} tries to share marbles equally among friends. Giving everybody {q} marbles, {a.he} lacks {d}; giving everybody {p}, {a.he} keeps {s} spare. How many friends are there?
test: Bananas for the monkeys at a zoo in Sydney: {p} per monkey leaves {s} bananas in the bucket, but {q} per monkey would need {d} bananas more. How many monkeys are there?
test: On a school trip in Texas, putting {p} pupils in each minibus leaves {s} pupils without a seat, and putting {q} in each minibus leaves {d} seats free. How many minibuses are there?
test: {s} books would be left over if each shelf took {p} books, while {d} books would be lacking if each shelf took {q} books. How many shelves are there?
test: A scout leader in Scotland gives out badges: with {q} badges per scout she is {d} short; with {p} per scout, {s} remain in the box. How many scouts are in the troop?
exercise: if every kid gets {p} pencils theres {s} spare, if every kid gets {q} we're {d} short. how many kids
exercise: {a} sharing candies — {p} each n {s} left over, {q} each n {a.he}'s {d} short. how many mates is that
exercise: {p} stickers each, {s+1} left — no wait, {s} left. {q} each, {d} short. how many kids then
exercise: teacher (she's got like 30 years in) gives out {p} books each, {s} left over; {q} each, {d} missing. how many students
test: Sam just added em all up, {s}+{d}+{p}. nah thats wrong. {p} each leaves {s}, {q} each leaves u {d} short. how many kids really
test: {q} cookies each n we're {d} cookies short, {p} each n {s} left over. how many children we got
test: {a} hands out {p} balloons per kid, {s} spare. tries {q} per kid — {d} short, oh and 2 popped earlier but forget those. how many kids
exercise: Grandad passed away and left a jar of old coins for his grandchildren. If each grandchild gets {p} coins, {s} coins are left over. If each gets {q} coins, {d} coins are missing. How many grandchildren are there?
exercise: mum's been let go so it's cheap sweets this year. {p} each, {s} left over. {q} each, {d} short. how many kids, use your brain
test: The teacher was economical with the truth when she said there were "plenty" of pencils: giving {q} pencils to every pupil leaves the class {d} pencils short, while {p} each leaves {s} spare. How many pupils are in the class?
exam: It is given that, were each child to receive {p} pencils, a surplus of {s} pencils would remain, whereas, were each child to receive {q} pencils, a deficiency of {d} pencils would arise. Determine the number of children.
exam: Ascertain the number of recipients among whom a stock of candies is to be distributed, given that an allocation of {p} candies per recipient leaves {s} candies undistributed, and that an allocation of {q} candies per recipient would require a further {d} candies.
exam: A quantity of books is to be apportioned equally among a number of pupils. Whereas an apportionment of {p} books per pupil results in a remainder of {s} books, an apportionment of {q} books per pupil results in a shortfall of {d} books. State the number of pupils.
exam: Pursuant to two alternative schemes of distribution of stickers among the members of a club, namely {p} per member, which leaves {s} stickers in excess, and {q} per member, which leaves {d} stickers wanting, determine the number of members of the club.
